{
  "dom_id": "step-q143000",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/a89c73fd1e50e3c2d28694a366c79c75ad5f1bbf63a5c1333a7d7b62f5a4a0e9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{TP}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$",
  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TP}=\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\n \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/e0e6351dfbef6dadf6a6fb179f1545622ddaeab24c2c06dee03c87821304695c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TR}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/02899ac9c536b0020514780db617e29977a5dc4daf60f42d80ed671883327fe3.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TP} & =\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =-d+b+a \\\\  & =a+b-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
